### 1.1 Catalogue mechanics (read firsthand)
- Digest §T04 (≈13–27 s): "a rounded panel labelled روزها ("Days") holds a grid of day numerals; most are struck through with diagonal slashes, two are ringed with a ⊗ mark, and dashed drop-lines connect two crossed dates to a timeline below where their labels are also struck out. A second panel (کارها, "Tasks") with empty checkboxes joins it. Draw-on order: grid → strikes → drop-lines → timeline labels." Why it works: ""after such-and-such a date it will work out" is refuted visually — the calendar itself is a record of dates that already failed."
- Style-library techniques-diagrams.md §T243: agrees on mechanics, window (P14 @ ≈13–27 s), evidence (ev/020 · ev/026). Tier DESCRIBED — OWED #108. No digest↔register disagreement.
- **Firsthand corrections to the digest prose (priced readings, binding on the build):** (1) The grid is **7 columns × 5 rows = 35 day numerals** (Persian numerals ۱–۳۵, right-to-left reading order), counted in ev/020 + census frames. (2) The "two ringed" are not simultaneous: a PLAIN ring first marks day 16 (census @17 s) and is later overstruck; a ⊗ (ring + cross) marks the day-23 cell by 19 s; a plain ring marks day 34 by 19 s and converts to ⊗ only in the final state (25 s) — ring lifecycle is three distinct beats, priced in §2. (3) The timeline + star figure + dashed arc are ALREADY present at the first calendar frame (17 s) with ONE drop-line (from day 16) and its label UNSTRUCK; the second drop-line, the first label's strike, and the second label arrive by 19 s. The digest's draw-on order (grid → strikes → drop-lines → timeline labels) is the CLAIM the build enforces for its own phases; the source's pre-assembled timeline/figure is named as set, not technique, in §2.2 deferrals. (4) The window head (13–17 s) is T242's bar chart and the tail (≥26.5 s) dissolves into the next scene (tiny population figures, panels dimming) — both are departures tabled in §2.3, not T243 lifecycle. (5) The figure's chest star CRACKS at 26 s — the next beat's opening glyph, deferred (§2.2).

